blog-webCLI is licensed per surface, because it has two:

  RUNTIME  lib/            MIT                       lib/LICENSE
           vendor/moment/  MIT (moment.js, upstream) vendor/moment/LICENSE

     The runtime is the generator and the renderer. A blog's generated pages
     carry a script built from it (webcli-live.js), so it ends up inside other
     people's pages. It is permissive so that publishing a blog with this tool
     never makes the blog a combined work of anything copyleft.

  TOOL     app/ cli/ blog/ index.html templates/ examples/ test/
           and everything else not listed above
                                        GPL-3.0-only   LICENSE-GPL-3.0

     The editor, the command line and the publish path run from
     blog.webcli.app or from a person's own machine. Nobody receives them
     inside another work, so copyleft costs no one anything.

  Third-party files, with their own licences, are listed in
  licences-inventory.json (generated by `node cli/inventory.js`); a build
  fails if one with no licence, or a copyleft one, reaches the runtime.

  A test asserts that the script a blog ships is built only from the runtime
  directories above (test/licence.test.js), so the split stays true.

  Themes and extensions are separate repositories with their own licences
  and are not affected.
